.TH ETHER 3
.SH NAME
ether \- Ethernet and Wi-Fi interfaces of the native kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind #l/ether0 /net/ether0
.B bind -a #l1 /net
.B bind -a #l2 /net

.BI /net/ether n /clone
.BI /net/ether n /addr
.BI /net/ether n /stats
.BI /net/ether n /ifstats
.BI /net/ether n / c /ctl
.BI /net/ether n / c /data
.BI /net/ether n / c /type
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ether
serves the native kernel's link-level network interfaces.
.B #l
is
.BR ether0 ,
wired Ethernet;
.B #l1
is
.BR ether1 ,
the Raspberry Pi's Wi-Fi;
.B #l2
and
.B #l3
are spares for USB adapters, used when
.B ether0
is taken (a Pi 4's GENET, a virtio card, a first adapter).
.B Ether1
with nothing behind it refuses to attach, so a kernel with no
radio has no
.B /net/ether1
rather than an empty one; a spare attaches, empty, until an adapter
binds it.
.PP
The files are those of every Inferno and Plan 9 network interface.
Opening
.B clone
makes a new conversation,
.IR c ,
and the descriptor is its
.B ctl
file; writing
.B "connect \fItype\fP"
to it selects the Ethernet type whose frames
.B data
will carry
.RB ( -1
for all), and
.BR promiscuous ,
.BR headersonly ,
.B "addmulti \fIaddr\fP"
and
.B "remmulti \fIaddr\fP"
have their usual meanings.
A read of
.B data
returns one whole frame, header included; a write sends one.
.B Addr
is the interface's address as twelve hexadecimal digits \- all zeros while
nothing drives the interface \- and
.B stats
the counters the kernel keeps (packets in and out, errors,
.BR link ,
.BR mbps ,
.BR addr ).
.B Ifstats
is the driver's own report.
.PP
The IP stack uses an interface by name:
.IP
.EX
bind ether /net/ether0
.EE
.PP
written to an
.B ipifc
(see
.IR ip (3)).
.SS "What is behind ether0"
Three different things, none of which a user of the files can see.
.TP
A USB Ethernet adapter (Raspberry Pi)
The board's LAN7515, a CDC ECM or NCM adapter, phone or gadget, or an
RNDIS one, is brought up by a
Limbo program,
.B /dis/etherusb.dis
\- registers, PHY negotiation, address \- which then hands the kernel
the adapter's two open bulk endpoints with
.RS
.IP
.EX
bind \fIfamily mac mbps burst inpath outpath\fP [\fIname\fB=\fIvalue\fR ...]
.EE
.PP
written to any
.B ctl
of
.B #l/ether0
or a spare.
.I Family
is
.BR lan78xx ,
.BR rndis ,
.B ecm
(a bulk transfer is a frame) or
.B ncm
(a transfer is an NTB-16 of frames),
naming the framing the kernel is to apply; the paths are resolved in the
writer's namespace.
The optional words are what the framing needs to know of the device:
.B maxpkt
(the bulk packet size; a frame or block that would end on a packet
boundary is sent a byte longer instead of needing a zero-length packet)
for ECM and NCM, and for NCM the device's
.B GET_NTB_PARAMETERS
answer \-
.B txmax
(the largest NTB it takes),
.B div
and
.B rem
(where in one a datagram may start),
.B align
(the NDP's alignment) and
.B maxdgrams
(datagrams per NTB, 0 for any).
A bind on an instance a kernel driver has is refused as in use, which is
how
.B etherusb
finds the first free one.
From then on frames move inside the kernel.
.B unbind
releases the endpoints.
An interface binds once at a time.
.RE
.TP
A kernel driver (QEMU's virt)
A virtio network device is driven wholly in the kernel and is live from
boot, its address already in
.BR addr ;
.IR osinit (8)
takes a non-zero address there to mean there is nothing to bring up.
.TP
Nothing
.B Addr
is zeros and
.B data
writes fail until a
.B bind
arrives.
.SS ether1
The CYW43455 radio, driven in the kernel.
Its
.B ctl
messages \- joining a network, keys \- and its firmware files are
described in
.IR wpa (8)
and
.BR /docs/WIFI-WPA2.md ;
.B scanbs
in a conversation's
.B ctl
asks for a scan.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/port/devether.c
.br
.B /os/port/netif.c
.br
.B /os/bcm2837/ether4330.c
.br
.B /os/virtio/ethervirtio.c
.br
.B /os/init/etherusb.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ip (3),
.IR osinit (8),
.IR wpa (8)
.SH BUGS
The link is taken to be up for as long as the interface is bound:
nothing watches the cable.
.br
On
.B ether0
with a USB adapter, two further
.B ctl
messages,
.B blackhole
and
.BR rxstats ,
and the contents of
.BR ifstats ,
are instruments left from measuring the receive path and may change.
